#!/usr/bin/env perl
use strict;
use warnings;
our $VERSION = '0.001';

# PODNAME: simpici-worker
# ABSTRACT: Pull exact-revision jobs over an outbound SSH connection

use Carp qw( croak );
use Fcntl qw( LOCK_EX LOCK_NB );
use Getopt::Long qw( GetOptions );
use Path::Tiny qw( path );
use SimpiCI::Runner;
use SimpiCI::Store;
use SimpiCI::Worker;

umask 0077;
my ($host, $root, $once, $executor);
my $timeout = 3600;
GetOptions('dispatcher=s' => \$host, 'root=s' => \$root, 'once' => \$once,
  'executor=s' => \$executor, 'timeout=i' => \$timeout)
  && $host && $root or croak 'Usage: simpici-worker --dispatcher USER@HOST --root DIR [--once]';
my $store = SimpiCI::Store->new(root => path($root))->prepare;
my $lock = $store->root->child('worker.lock')->opena_raw;
flock($lock, LOCK_EX | LOCK_NB) or croak 'simpici-worker already running';
my $worker = SimpiCI::Worker->new(
  host => $host, store => $store,
  runner => SimpiCI::Runner->new(store => $store, timeout => $timeout,
    $executor ? (runner_script => path($executor)) : ())
);
while (1) {
  # One line for a cycle that failed: what follows the first line of an
  # error is a backtrace, with the arguments of every call in it.
  eval { $worker->once; 1 }
    or warn 'simpici-worker: '.( ( split /\n/, $@ )[0] // 'unknown error' )."\n";
  last if $once;
  sleep 10;
}

=head1 NAME

simpici-worker - outbound-only SimpiCI worker

=head1 SYNOPSIS

  simpici-worker --dispatcher simpici@ci.example --root /var/lib/simpici-worker

=head1 DESCRIPTION

Runs the shared container executor on exact detached checkouts. Install the
SSH host key ahead of time. The remote account must force simpici-dispatch
with a fixed config and worker identity and disable all SSH forwarding.
A local lock prevents overlapping worker processes. Completed work is saved
before upload and retried without rebuilding when the connection fails.

A saved completion, C<completion.json> below C<--root>, is sent again in
every cycle until the dispatcher answers, and no work is claimed while one
waits. A connection that fails, a dispatcher that ends with an error and an
answer that did not arrive whole are written to standard error as

  simpici-worker: SimpiCI::Worker dispatcher connection failed at ...
  simpici-worker: SimpiCI::Worker invalid dispatcher response at ...

and leave the file where it is. A dispatcher that ends with an error says
which kind of error on the line above, in one of five fixed phrases and
nothing else, such as

  simpici-dispatch: configuration unusable

The details are not sent to a worker. The operator of the dispatcher finds
them in F<dispatch.log> below its state root, see L<simpici-dispatch>. A completion the dispatcher refuses for
good, because the lease of the run is over, the run is claimed under another
worker name or token, or the dispatcher does not know the run, is not sent
again. It is moved to C<rejected/E<lt>runE<gt>.json> below C<--root>, one
line names it on standard error,

  SimpiCI::Worker completion of run 7 rejected by the dispatcher: expired claim; kept as rejected/7.json

and the next cycle claims work. The file is the completion as it was sent:
the result, the claim token and the log, in which the secret values of the
claim are redacted. C<simpici-worker> never reads or removes a file below
C<rejected/>; see L<SimpiCI::Worker/Delivering a completion>.

A run whose checkout or executor is ended by a signal is reported with the
state C<signalled> and the exit code 128 plus the signal, never as a
success; see L<SimpiCI::Runner/run>.

A claim the worker cannot execute, such as an event it refuses, is reported
to the dispatcher as a failed run with exit code 125. The reason is the last
line of the uploaded log:

  SimpiCI::Worker run 7 aborted: invalid event in claim

The log is published, so the reason is one of a list of fixed phrases and
never what the error said: C<invalid event in claim>,
C<invalid secrets in claim>, C<invalid secret name in claim>,
C<invalid secret value in claim>, C<invalid timeout in claim>,
C<cannot write secret files>, C<cannot create temporary directory>,
C<run supervisor failed> and, for everything else, C<internal error>.
L<SimpiCI::Worker/abort_reasons> says what each stands for. No path below
C<--root>, no file or line of the installation and no value of the claim is
in the log for it. What the error said is written to standard error, after
the same line:

  SimpiCI::Worker run 7 aborted: invalid event in claim: SimpiCI::Event ref must start with refs/ at ...
  SimpiCI::Worker run 7 aborted: run supervisor failed: SimpiCI::Store->write_json cannot publish /var/lib/simpici-worker/public/runs/7.json: Read-only file system at ...

A claim whose secrets are not one C<NAME=VALUE> line each is refused the same
way, with C<invalid secret name in claim> or C<invalid secret value in claim>
as the reason: an undefined or empty value, a reference, a value with a line
end, a name that is no C<CICD_E<lt>NAMEE<gt>> or C<E<lt>NAMEE<gt>_TOKEN>.

Standard error is the log of the operator: it names files below C<--root>
and where in the installation an error was raised, and it has one line for
one event. A cycle that failed is the first line of its error after
C<simpici-worker: >, as in the examples above and below; the other lines
begin with the class that says them.

No secret file is written for a refused claim, and the secret files of a run
are removed whichever way it ends, as long as the worker lives to do it. A
worker that is killed during a run leaves C<secrets/E<lt>runE<gt>/> below
C<--root>. The next start removes everything below C<secrets/> before it
connects to the dispatcher, and so does every later cycle, and writes one
line per entry to standard error:

  SimpiCI::Worker removed orphaned secret files: secrets/7

The run of the killed worker is not repeated: it keeps its lease at the
dispatcher until that expires and is then C<interrupted>. If an entry cannot
be removed, the worker reports C<cannot remove secret files> with the path
in every cycle and neither delivers a completion nor claims work until it is
gone.

=head2 What is kept below --root

C<--root> is the state of this worker alone: the worker removes every log
and every checkout it finds there. A root that holds the state of a
dispatcher or of a C<simpicid>, which it knows by C<queue/>, C<claims/> or
C<state/>, is refused in every cycle and nothing is removed from it:

  simpici-worker: SimpiCI::Worker root is the state of a dispatcher or a daemon, it has queue/: /var/lib/simpici at ...

A root that only C<simpici> made runs in has none of these and is not
recognised; do not use one.

A run leaves its report, C<public/runs/E<lt>runE<gt>.json>, and nothing
else. Its log, C<public/runs/E<lt>runE<gt>.log>, is what the jobs printed,
unredacted, and exists only while the run lasts: it is removed once the
completion is saved, which carries the log with the secret values of the
claim redacted. Its checkout C<work/E<lt>runE<gt>/>, the temporary files of
the executor C<tmp/E<lt>runE<gt>/> with the output, the outputs and the
artifacts of every job, and C<runs/E<lt>runE<gt>/> are removed at the same
time, whichever way the run ended and before the completion is sent.

What a job wrote as another account than the worker cannot always be
removed by the worker. It then says so once,

  SimpiCI::Worker cannot remove run files: tmp/7

goes on with the next claim and tries again in every cycle. A log that
cannot be removed stops it instead, like a secret file: it reports C<cannot
remove the log of a run> with the path in every cycle until the log is gone.

A worker that is killed during a run leaves all of this. The next start
removes it before it connects to the dispatcher, with one line per entry,

  SimpiCI::Worker removed orphaned run files: public/runs/7.log
  SimpiCI::Worker removed orphaned run files: work/7

and so does the first start of this version on the state of an earlier one,
which kept every log and every checkout: copy what is to be kept before the
upgrade. See L<SimpiCI::Worker/What a run leaves>.

=head2 Ending the worker

C<TERM>, C<INT> and C<HUP> end the worker, unless it was started to ignore
one of them, as C<HUP> under C<nohup>. Between two runs they end it at
once. From the claim to the end of a run the worker first ends the run, at
whatever point it is: no further command of it is started, the process
group of the executor is sent C<TERM>, and C<KILL> after five seconds if it
is still there, and the containers of the run are killed and removed. The
worker then
saves the completion of the run, with the state C<signalled> and the exit
code 128 plus the signal, removes the secret files and the files of the run
and ends by the signal it received. Standard error gets

  SimpiCI::Runner removed containers of run 7: 2
  SimpiCI::Runner run 7 stopped by signal TERM

the first line only if the executor did not get to remove them itself. The
completion is not sent on the way out; the next start sends it. If the
lease of the run is over by then, the dispatcher refuses it and it is kept
under C<rejected/>.

A worker that is killed with C<KILL> cannot do that. A process it started
beside the executor notices that the worker is gone, ends the process group
and removes the containers in the same way, within seconds. It holds the
lock of the worker until it is done: a worker started in that time ends
with C<simpici-worker already running> and has to be started again. The
secret files, the log and the files of the run stay until that next start
removes them.

The containers are found by a label that only this C<--root> has: every
container the executor starts carries
C<simpici.instance=E<lt>instanceE<gt>> and
C<simpici.run=E<lt>instanceE<gt>.E<lt>runE<gt>>, with the random value kept
in the file C<instance> below C<--root>. No other container is ever killed
or removed, not that of another worker on the same daemon either. While a
run lasts, C<containers/E<lt>runE<gt>> names its label. A file that is
still there at a start names a run whose containers nobody removed, for
example because the worker was killed together with everything it had
started. The worker then asks docker for the containers of its instance,
removes them before anything else and says how many there were:

  SimpiCI::Worker removed orphaned containers: 2

If docker cannot be asked, it reports C<cannot remove orphaned containers>
with the reason in every cycle and neither delivers a completion nor claims
work until it could; the secret files, the log and the files of the run are
removed all the same. A container that a job itself started through the
Docker socket of a build or publish job carries no such label and is not
found. See L<SimpiCI::Runner/Ending a run>.

=cut
